<title>COLOMBIA: Strike forces Colombia to tap coal inventories.</title>
<headline>Strike forces Colombia to tap coal inventories.</headline>
<dateline>BOGOTA 1997-04-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mines and Energy Minister Rodrigo Villamizar said a nationwide strike by Colombia's main truck drivers' union was forcing  coal exporters to dip into inventories at port to meet their export commitments.</p>
<p>&quot;The truckers' strike has cut down on the movement of coal, but there are sufficient reserves in the ports to meet all commitments,&quot; Villamizar told reporters.</p>
<p>He said coal inventories were only sufficient &quot;for about 15 days,&quot; however.</p>
<p>The head of the Colombian Truck Drivers' Association (ACC) said earlier Tuesday that he saw no early end to the work stoppage, which began at midnight Saturday.</p>
<p>The strike was called by the ACC to press for fulfillment, by the government and private transportation companies, of terms of an accord that ended a crippling, 11-day work stoppage last October.</p>
<p>((-- Juan Guillermo Londono, Bogota newsroom, 571 610 7944))</p>
